<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Limassol</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lemesos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Limassol is a port city on the southern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and the island's second-largest city. It grew near the ancient city-kingdom of Amathus and became important in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a> landed there in 1191, defeated Isaac Komnenos and married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berengaria-of-navarre">Berengaria of Navarre</a> in the town. Today it is a centre of shipping, tourism and the wine trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/limassol.jpg" alt="Limassol" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Στέφανος Νικολάου · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyscrapers_in_Limassol.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Limassol lies on the southern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, between the ancient city-kingdoms of Amathus and Kourion, both founded around 1100 BCE. Its name comes from the ancient Greek Nemessos, meaning one found in the middle, which probably refers to that position; it later became Lemesos. The historic centre is around the medieval Limassol Castle and the Old Port. Human activity in the area goes back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, around 7000 BCE. Ancient writers and geographers do not mention the founding of the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town's history is bound up with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a>. Richard the Lionheart was travelling to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> when a storm brought the ship of his fiancée Berengaria and his sister Joan to Limassol. Isaac Komnenos, the renegade Byzantine governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, called the noblewomen ashore, intending to hold them for ransom. When they refused, he denied them fresh water, and they put out to sea. Richard took Isaac captive when he arrived, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-normans">Anglo-Normans</a> took over the island, ending the long Byzantine dominion of Cyprus. Richard married Berengaria on Cyprus, and the castle is the traditional site of the wedding, held in 1191.</p><p>Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-cornaro">Catherine Cornaro</a> sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1489. The Venetians wanted chiefly to tax the island, and travellers of the 16th century remarked on the poor condition of the local people. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took Limassol and its castle in 1538, and the Venetian governor, after retaking it, ordered it demolished to prevent its seizure, which was completed in 1567 and 1568. The Ottomans invaded Cyprus in 1570 and took Limassol in July without resistance. They built a new fort on the remains of the castle in 1590, whose cells were used as a prison until 1950.</p><p>The British took over the administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> in 1878. Their first governor of Limassol, Colonel Warren, had the roads cleaned and repaired, docks built for loading ships anchored offshore and trees planted. Lanterns lit the centre from the 1880s, and electricity replaced them in 1912. After the 1974 Turkish invasion the Turkish Cypriot inhabitants moved north, and many Greek Cypriot refugees settled in the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Limassol is the second-largest urban area in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and its main port, a role that passed to it from Famagusta in 1974. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> live around the Sourp Kevork church, and the Laniteio Lyceum, founded in 1819 as the Greek School, is the oldest lyceum in the city. The ruins of Amathus, with its Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a>, lie about 11 kilometres to the east. Kolossi Castle, built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> in the 13th century and rebuilt in 1454, stands about 11 kilometres to the west. </p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Limassol” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During his conquest of Byzantine-ruled Cyprus on the way to the Third Crusade, Richard I married Berengaria of Navarre at <b>Limassol</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-12">12 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In May 1191 ships of the Third Crusade carrying Richard I's sister and his betrothed Berengaria were stranded at <b>Limassol</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/cyprus-under-isaac-komnenos">Cyprus under Isaac Komnenos</a></li><li><blockquote>Amathus was founded c. 1050 BCE on a hill near modern <b>Limassol</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-amathus">Kingdom of Amathus</a></li></ul></section>
